In this lesson we'll look at how to tackle composite shapes, which are just two or more simple shapes joined together — usually rectangles, but sometimes a triangle or a curved bit. The big idea is simple: split the shape into pieces you recognise, work out each piece, then combine. For area, you've got two options. You can add the pieces — break it into familiar shapes and add their areas together. Or you can subtract the gap — start with a big rectangle that covers everything, then take away the empty corner. Both give the same answer, so just pick whichever needs fewer, simpler pieces. For perimeter, picture yourself walking around the outside edge with a tape measure and adding up every length you touch. Don't forget any internal edge that sits between two pieces — that counts too. And if the whole shape is rectangular, opposite sides are equal, so you only need two adjacent sides to figure out the rest. Finally, a quick word on units. Pick whichever makes sense: square metres for a garden bed, square centimetres for a sheet of paper, and hectares for a farm, where one hectare equals ten thousand square metres. Now let's see it in action.